# Boundary localization (local-mode View B)

**What this is.** Each row in `boundary_localization.tsv` is one
FLARE switch (ancestry transition) on a haplotype. For each, the
collector found the nearest popout switch and recorded (a) the
bp distance to it and (b) whether the flanking RF labels on
both sides match. The left panel is the log-scale distribution
of those distances; the right panel is the matched / unmatched
breakdown for the flanking labels.

**How to read this chart.** A left panel sharply peaked near
`10^3 bp` means popout typically places its switches within a kb
of FLARE's — local-mode tracking is fine-grained. A flat or
right-skewed distribution means popout and FLARE disagree about
where transitions happen, even when they agree about the labels
involved. The right-panel "matched" fraction tells you how often
the two tools agree on *what* is on either side, regardless of
*where* the switch sits.

